# Logs — always through `utils/logger`

## Rule
- App code never calls `console.*` (`log`, `info`, `warn`, `error`, `debug`…). It writes through `utils/logger.ts`:
  a channel logger (`authLogger`, `apiLogger`…), a helper when a message repeats (`logAuth.login`,
  `logApi.request`…), or `loggerInstance` for a one-off without a channel.
- `utils/logger.ts` is the only file that reaches the console, through react-native-logs (`consoleTransport`).
- Node scripts under `scripts/` are command-line tools, not app code: they keep `console` / `process.stdout`.

## Why
- One place decides what prints: a level per channel in `LOGGER_CONFIG` (`debug`, `info`, or `false` to silence
  it), everything in dev, errors only in release, with the channel's colour and the time. `console` bypasses all of it
  and prints in release too.

## A message without a channel
- Add the channel to `utils/logger.ts`: its level in `LOGGER_CONFIG`, its colour in `extensionColors`, then
  `export const {name}Logger = createLogger('NAME')`, and helpers (`log{Name}.…`) when the same message comes back.
- Give it a card in the debug menu's Logs lab (`components/_devDebugMenu/DebugLogs.tsx`), which writes through every
  channel and says where each call lands.

## Audit
- `F-LOG-01` (error): a `console.*` call outside `utils/logger.ts`.
- Unused channels and helpers of `utils/logger.ts` are not reported by `F-DEAD-01`: the stack ships them for the
  app to use as it grows.

## Related
- Biome: `noConsole` is an error in the app's `biome.json`, off only for `scripts/**`, so the editor flags a call as
  it is typed; the audit (`F-LOG-01`) reports any left.
